🔥 Question 1: Median of Sliding Window (HARD)
🟢 Problem Statement

Given:

An integer array nums

A sliding window size k

For every window of size k, return the median.

📌 Example
Input:
nums = [1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7]
k = 3

Output:
[1,-1,-1,3,5,6]

🟢 Step 1: Understanding the Problem

Window size = 3

Windows are:

Window	Elements	Sorted	Median
1	[1,3,-1]	[-1,1,3]	1
2	[3,-1,-3]	[-3,-1,3]	-1
3	[-1,-3,5]	[-3,-1,5]	-1
4	[-3,5,3]	[-3,3,5]	3
5	[5,3,6]	[3,5,6]	5
6	[3,6,7]	[3,6,7]	6
🟢 Why Brute Force Won’t Work?

Brute force:

For each window → sort → find median

Time Complexity: O(nk log k) ❌

For n = 10^5 → Too Slow

🟢 Optimal Approach: Two Heaps

We maintain:

🔵 Max Heap → Left half (smaller numbers)

🔴 Min Heap → Right half (larger numbers)

Rules:

Size difference ≤ 1

MaxHeap contains smaller half

Median:

If k odd → top of maxHeap

If k even → average of two tops

🟢 Algorithm

Create two heaps:

maxHeap (small numbers)

minHeap (large numbers)

Insert first k elements

Balance heaps

Calculate median

Slide window:

Remove outgoing element

Add new element

Rebalance

Store median

🟢 Code (Java – Interview Standard)
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
    PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

    public double[] medianSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
        double[] result = new double[nums.length - k + 1];

        for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            // Add number
            addNum(nums[i]);

            // Remove element outside window
            if(i >= k)
                removeNum(nums[i - k]);

            // Store median
            if(i >= k - 1)
                result[i - k + 1] = getMedian(k);
        }
        return result;
    }

    private void addNum(int num) {
        if(maxHeap.isEmpty() || num <= maxHeap.peek())
            maxHeap.offer(num);
        else
            minHeap.offer(num);

        balanceHeaps();
    }

    private void removeNum(int num) {
        if(num <= maxHeap.peek())
            maxHeap.remove(num);
        else
            minHeap.remove(num);

        balanceHeaps();
    }

    private void balanceHeaps() {
        if(maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size() + 1)
            minHeap.offer(maxHeap.poll());

        else if(minHeap.size() > maxHeap.size())
            maxHeap.offer(minHeap.poll());
    }

    private double getMedian(int k) {
        if(k % 2 == 1)
            return maxHeap.peek();
        return ((double)maxHeap.peek() + minHeap.peek()) / 2.0;
    }
}

🟢 Line by Line Explanation
1️⃣ Create Two Heaps
PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();


maxHeap → biggest element at top

minHeap → smallest element at top

2️⃣ Sliding Loop
for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++)


We iterate entire array

3️⃣ Add Element
addNum(nums[i]);


Insert new window element

4️⃣ Remove Old Element
if(i >= k)
    removeNum(nums[i - k]);


Once window crosses k → remove old element

5️⃣ Store Median
if(i >= k - 1)
    result[i - k + 1] = getMedian(k);


When first full window formed → start storing

6️⃣ Balance Heaps

Ensures:

|size(maxHeap) - size(minHeap)| <= 1

🟢 Dry Run With Example

Input:

nums = [1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7]
k = 3

First 3 elements:

Insert 1 → maxHeap = [1]

Insert 3 → minHeap = [3]

Insert -1 → maxHeap = [1,-1]

Median = 1 ✅

Next:

Remove 1
Add -3

Heaps balanced → Median = -1 ✅

Continue…

Final Output:

[1,-1,-1,3,5,6]

🟢 Time Complexity
Operation	Complexity
Add	O(log k)
Remove	O(k) (worst case in Java PQ)
Overall	O(n log k)

In interviews you can improve removal using Lazy Deletion (HashMap)
